# How the plan will be used

{{ psc.plan_usage_guidance() }}

The `SAFETY_PLAN.md` you produce will be provided to each agent instance as the Level {{ details|length + 1 }} plan, alongside the existing {% if details|length == 1 %}Level 1 plan{% else %}Level 1-{{ details|length }} plans{% endif %}.  Don't repeat information from the pre-existing planning documents, since each agent will see them alongside your new one.

Don't excessively subdivide the super-step into large numbers of sub-steps; limit yourself to 5-10 at most. The CRISP harness will recursively call this subdivision procedure if any of the resulting sub-steps is still too large.


# `SAFETY_PLAN.md` output format

`SAFETY_PLAN.md` must contain these two sections:

1.  `## Conventions` -- the cross-cutting decisions relevant to multiple sub-steps: {{ psc.safety_plan_conventions_examples }}.  Some of these may be covered more generally in the pre-existing planning documents; don't repeat those generalities, but instead note the specifics of how those conventions apply to the code covered by the target super-step, and especially note any exceptions or new conventions that are specific to this code (e.g. if the existing plan says `*mut c_char` should be mapped to `String`, but this module has several `*mut c_char`s for which `OsString` is more appropriate, note the modified convention here).

2.  `## Step-by-step guide` -- break down the target super-step into sub-steps that can be carried out one by one.  For each sub-step, note all of the following: the files, functions, types, and/or fields that should be made safe during that step; a general description of the migration strategy; dependencies on previous steps; a table mapping each struct field and function argument/return that contains raw pointers to its recommended safe type with a one-line rationale; pitfalls and risky edge cases; and semantic done-criteria.  Group same-shaped migrations into a single unit: if migrating `f` to `String` implies the same change in `g`, plan them together.


